course: dictionary
lesson: 5934
title: Names of people and places
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are names. They name one particular person, place or people, and they begin with a capital letter. Masai is one of these words. The Maasai (also spelled Masai) are a people who live in Kenya and Tanzania, in East Africa.
explain: Context shows you which kind of name it is. «Each year more than a million wildebeest, with zebras and gazelles, follow the rain and fresh grass in a loop round the Serengeti and the Masai Mara.» The Masai Mara sits in a line with the Serengeti, and the animals move around it, so it must be a place: a wildlife reserve in Kenya.
explain: The Maasai are a living people with their own lives today, not a story from a book. Write their name with a capital letter, and describe them with respect, as real people in the world now.
fact: A proper noun is the name of one particular person, place or people, and it begins with a capital letter.
fact: Masai (also spelled Maasai) is the name of a people of Kenya and Tanzania.
fact: The Maasai are famous for cattle herding and their bright red cloth.
fact: The Masai Mara is a wildlife reserve in Kenya.
fact: «The great migration is real: over a million wildebeest, with zebras and gazelles, move round the Serengeti and Masai Mara every year following rain and fresh grass.»
exercise: «The Masai Mara is a wildlife reserve.» What is the Masai Mara? | *A wildlife reserve in Kenya | A kind of cattle | A people of Tanzania | A bright red cloth
exercise: The Maasai are famous for what? | Ocean fishing | *Cattle herding and bright red cloth | Snow sports | Making glass
exercise: Why does Masai start with a capital letter? | It is an adjective | It is at the start of a sentence | *It names a particular people or place | It is a very long word
test: «The Masai Mara is a wildlife reserve in Kenya.» Which country is it in? | Tanzania | *Kenya | Australia | Scotland
test: Which animals follow the rain and fresh grass round the Masai Mara? | *Wildebeest, zebras and gazelles | Penguins and seals | Pandas and koalas | Camels and goats
exam: How should you write about the Masai? | Call them a made-up tribe from a film | Copy costumes from a picture to make the page look colourful | Write them off as a story from long ago | *Describe them as a living people with their own lives today
